1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,372
Here's how to reverse engineer HubSpot from a written spec.

2
00:00:03,372 --> 00:00:07,250
Claude passed all twenty-seven checks, and found eight gaps in my spec.

3
00:00:08,017 --> 00:00:09,809
We gave Claude one Markdown file.

4
00:00:10,023 --> 00:00:12,967
It copies the part of HubSpot a small sales team actually uses.

5
00:00:13,265 --> 00:00:15,484
Contacts and companies, each with an owner.

6
00:00:15,783 --> 00:00:18,001
One deal pipeline, with stage probabilities.

7
00:00:18,300 --> 00:00:20,598
A log of every call, email and meeting.

8
00:00:20,806 --> 00:00:24,138
And CSV import, with a preview before anything is saved.

9
00:00:25,217 --> 00:00:26,753
The core rule is the follow-up.

10
00:00:26,966 --> 00:00:29,334
Every contact always has a next follow-up date.

11
00:00:29,526 --> 00:00:31,382
It can be moved, but never cleared.

12
00:00:31,702 --> 00:00:34,390
Overdue contacts sit at the top, oldest first.

13
00:00:34,646 --> 00:00:37,953
And logging a call always sets the next date, three days out by default.

14
00:00:38,145 --> 00:00:41,891
So Grace Kim, six days overdue, is the first name Sam sees.

15
00:00:42,983 --> 00:00:45,457
Claude built it in four phases, one prompt each.

16
00:00:45,713 --> 00:00:48,529
The foundation, with the team, companies and contacts.

17
00:00:48,785 --> 00:00:51,793
Activities, follow-ups, the Today screen and search.

18
00:00:52,071 --> 00:00:53,265
Deals and the pipeline.

19
00:00:53,521 --> 00:00:56,184
Then import, export and the daily digest.

20
00:00:56,371 --> 00:01:01,190
After every phase, the acceptance checks ran in a real browser, from fresh seed data.

21
00:01:02,283 --> 00:01:03,179
Here's what it built.

22
00:01:03,478 --> 00:01:07,019
The contacts list puts late follow-ups first, with the days overdue.

23
00:01:07,275 --> 00:01:10,539
The pipeline board totals every stage, open and weighted.

24
00:01:10,816 --> 00:01:13,158
Logging a call moves the next follow-up a week out.

25
00:01:13,323 --> 00:01:16,800
The import preview flags duplicates and errors before anything is saved.

26
00:01:16,992 --> 00:01:19,082
And on a phone, Today shows who to call.

27
00:01:20,183 --> 00:01:25,959
In the final run, on a fresh database, all twenty-seven checks passed, along with ninety-one automated tests.

28
00:01:27,050 --> 00:01:29,120
But the more useful result is what broke.

29
00:01:29,397 --> 00:01:31,552
The build exposed eight gaps in the spec.

30
00:01:31,786 --> 00:01:33,386
Places where Claude had to guess.

31
00:01:33,749 --> 00:01:38,118
Owner-only buttons were hidden, but the checks expected a colleague to try them and see the refusal.

32
00:01:38,307 --> 00:01:41,400
Unarchiving a contact with no follow-up date had no rule.

33
00:01:41,720 --> 00:01:45,133
And the morning digest drifted with daylight saving on a UTC scheduler.

34
00:01:45,389 --> 00:01:47,432
Every gap is now fixed in the spec.

35
00:01:48,517 --> 00:01:50,992
Passing every check isn't the same as production-ready.

36
00:01:51,248 --> 00:01:56,389
We didn't test real sign-in, sending email, the scheduled morning run, real phones, or hosting.

37
00:01:56,624 --> 00:01:58,670
Those are yours to check before a team relies on it.

38
00:01:59,750 --> 00:02:01,115
So is it worth building?

39
00:02:01,499 --> 00:02:03,846
HubSpot's free CRM covers up to two users.

40
00:02:04,059 --> 00:02:06,576
Paid seats start at fifteen dollars per seat per month.

41
00:02:06,790 --> 00:02:09,452
Pipedrive has no free tier, and starts at fourteen.

42
00:02:09,597 --> 00:02:14,163
If you rely on email sync, sequences or forecasting, pay for HubSpot.

43
00:02:14,461 --> 00:02:20,337
If your team works from contacts, deals and follow-ups, you mostly need the list, the board, and the reminder.

44
00:02:21,417 --> 00:02:22,761
Everything is in one file.

45
00:02:22,996 --> 00:02:29,824
The data model, twenty-one rules, seed data, four phase prompts, and all twenty-seven checks, with the eight fixes already in.

46
00:02:30,700 --> 00:02:34,191
The full spec is free at personal software factory dot com.

47
00:02:34,191 --> 00:02:36,321
If you build it, tell me what broke.

48
00:02:36,321 --> 00:02:36,794
I'm Sam.

49
00:02:36,794 --> 00:02:37,800
See you tomorrow.
